  "full_text": "Hello and welcome back to JD Science. Today we are exploring the nervous system. It lets us react to our surroundings. It contains the brain, spinal cord, neurons and receptors. In a conscious action, a stimulus is detected by a receptor. Sensory neurons carry the impulse to the spinal cord and brain. The brain decides on a response and motor neurons carry the impulse to an effector. In many spinal reflexes, the immediate response is coordinated through the spinal cord without waiting for a conscious decision. Sensory neurons send the impulse to a relay neuron in the spinal cord which sends it to a motor neuron. This is much faster. Neurons don't touch its synapses. Chemicals called neurotransmitters cross the gap. Eyes focus light using the lens and cornea. A reflex arc begins when a reflex arc begins when a receptor detects a stimulus. An impulse travels along a sensory neuron to the central nervous system where it may pass through a relay neuron before traveling along a motor neuron to an effector. Reflexes are automatic and can be rapid because they do not require a conscious decision before the response. The brain can still receive information about the event. At a chemical synapse, neurotransmitter molecules diffuse across the synaptic cleft and bind to receptors. As you revise this topic, try to explain the key process in your own words. Focus on what happens, why it happens and how the structures involved help the process work. Now pause the video and try this exam style question. Describe the pathway of a reflex arc in order. Here is a model answer. Receptor, sensory neuron, relay neuron in the spinal cord,",
